Hi [Manager],
I pulled the Illinois EPA Source Water Assessment Program (SWAP) factsheets f=
or the eight community water systems on our list: Farmer City, Springerton, B=
artlett, Enfield, Crossville, Weldon, Norris City and Waynesville. I then scr=
eened every listed well against our three criteria. The workbook is attached.=
 The "All Wells" tab has all 33 wells, a filter on each criterion, and a "MEE=
TS ALL CRITERIA?" column. The "Potential Wells" tab has only the wells that p=
ass.
Bottom line
7 of the 33 wells meet all three criteria. All 7 are in DeWitt County: 4 at F=
armer City, 2 at Weldon and 1 at Waynesville. My top recommendation is the Fa=
rmer City wellfield, led by Well #9 (WL47648). Weldon Wells #3 and #4 are the=
 secondary option.
Screening criteria applied
Depth from 160 to 200 ft, inclusive.
Aquifer description is Sand & Gravel.
Active well: the Well Description does not contain "abandoned", "inactive", "=
disconnected", "emergency" or "sealed".
A well must pass all three to be recommended. I did not use the IEPA Status c=
ode (A/I) as a criterion. Bartlett Wells #4 and #6, for example, are coded "A=
" but described as EMERGENCY, so they are excluded.
Recommended wells (in priority order)
Rank | Water System | Well ID / Description | Depth (ft) | Aquifer | Pumpage*=
 | Key note
1 | Farmer City | WL47648 =E2=80=93 Well 9 | 188 | Sand & Gravel (0101) | 26,=
478,500 | Highest recorded pumpage of all qualifying wells. Listed by IEPA as=
 an active supply well.
2 | Farmer City | WL01130 =E2=80=93 Well 11 | 192 | Sand & Gravel (0101) | 0 =
| IEPA narrative lists it as a supply well, but recorded pumpage is 0. Confir=
m output.
3 | Farmer City | WL47647 =E2=80=93 Well 10 | 181 | Sand & Gravel (0101) | 0 =
| Supply well per narrative. Recorded pumpage is 0. Iron and manganese above =
groundwater standards (natural).
4 | Farmer City | WL47646 =E2=80=93 Well 7 | 180 | Sand & Gravel (0101) | 21,=
976,200 | Passes the screen, but the IEPA narrative calls Well #7 inactive. M=
ust verify.
5 | Weldon | WL45047 =E2=80=93 Well 3 | 167 | Sand & Gravel (0909) | 5,500,00=
0 | Small system (about 39,000 gpd). IEPA rates it susceptible to IOC/VOC/SOC=
 contamination.
6 | Weldon | WL45048 =E2=80=93 Well 4 | 163 | Sand & Gravel (0909) | 3,503,04=
0 | Small system. No well-specific contamination finding.
7 | Waynesville | WL40006 =E2=80=93 Well 6 | 162 | Sand & Gravel (0909) | 4,3=
19,500 | Smallest system (about 27,000 gpd). Leaking UST remediation site 50 =
ft from the well.
*Pumpage as published by IEPA. The factsheet does not label units. System tot=
als roughly match each narrative's average daily production x 365, so I read =
these as gallons per year. That is my inference, not an IEPA statement.
Why Farmer City is the top option
Most depth: 4 qualifying wells (180=E2=80=93192 ft) in one sand-and-gravel we=
llfield. Weldon has 2 and Waynesville has 1.
Largest system among the qualifying towns: about 257,000 gallons/day average =
to a population of 2,450 (IEPA narrative). Weldon is about 39,000 gpd and Way=
nesville about 27,000 gpd. A larger system is more likely to have spare capac=
ity or interconnection options.
Well #9 has the highest recorded pumpage of any qualifying well (26.5 million=
, presumed gallons/year).
Water quality is manageable for an electrolysis feed. The raw water has natur=
al iron, manganese and arsenic (Well #9 arsenic 94 ppb raw). Finished arsenic=
 is 2.3 ppb, below the standard. Iron in finished water was 2,700=E2=80=933,2=
00 ppb in 1998, above the 1,000 ppb standard cited by IEPA. Our RO/deionizati=
on pretreatment will need to handle iron and manganese fouling.
Systems that did not produce a viable well
Springerton, Enfield, Crossville: every listed well is described as ABANDONED=
. Enfield's wells are also Shallow Bedrock at 353=E2=80=93410 ft.
Bartlett: no qualifying well. Wells are sealed, abandoned, disconnected or em=
ergency-only, and most are bedrock. Wells #1 and #2 are exactly 200 ft but Sh=
allow Bedrock and SEALED. Bartlett buys about 50% of its water from Elgin.
Norris City: two active sand-and-gravel wells, but they are too shallow (97 a=
nd 124 ft).
Weldon Well #6 (350 ft) and Waynesville Well #8 (207 ft) are active sand-and-=
gravel wells but fall outside the depth range. If we relax the depth criterio=
n, they would be the next candidates.
Items to resolve before we commit
Farmer City Well #7 conflict. The well table shows Status A, but the narrativ=
e says "Inactive Wells #4, #6, and #7." The narrative also lists Well #2 as a=
 supply well even though the table marks it ABANDONED. Parts of the factsheet=
 are likely out of date. We should confirm the current well inventory with Fa=
rmer City's water department.
Ownership and access. These are municipal public-supply wells. Our options ar=
e (a) a bulk water purchase agreement with the city or village, or (b) a new =
project well completed in the same sand-and-gravel aquifer nearby. IEPA remov=
es well locations from public factsheets, so siting needs the full SWAP repor=
t from local officials.
Regulatory trigger. A new project well (or group of wells on one property) ab=
le to pump 100,000 gallons/day or more is a high-capacity well under the Illi=
nois Water Use Act of 1983. The county Soil and Water Conservation District m=
ust be notified before construction, and it requests technical review from th=
e Illinois State Water Survey and Illinois State Geological Survey (CMAP summ=
ary, cited below).
Project water demand. We have not set the facility size, so I have not compar=
ed demand to well yield. For scale: DOE puts the stoichiometric minimum at 2.=
36 gal of water per kg H2. Its PEM direct-consumption estimate is 2.4=E2=80=
=938 gal/kg H2, depending on cooling design (higher end with evaporative cool=
ing). Withdrawals are higher than consumption because of RO reject. For examp=
le, at 1,000 kg H2/day, direct consumption would be about 2,400=E2=80=938,000=
 gal/day. That is small next to Farmer City's 257,000 gpd, but sizeable for W=
eldon or Waynesville. We should rerun this comparison once the electrolyzer c=
apacity is fixed.
Data age. The SWAP narratives cite Well Site Surveys from 1989=E2=80=931991 a=
nd sampling from the 1980s=E2=80=932000s. Pumpage values have no reporting ye=
ar. We should request current pumpage and water-quality data (e.g., recent Co=
nsumer Confidence Reports) before final selection.
Recommended next steps
Contact Farmer City's water department to confirm the status of Wells #7, #9,=
 #10 and #11, spare capacity, and willingness to discuss a supply agreement.
Ask Weldon the same questions as a backup.
Have our process engineer confirm design water demand and the RO/DI pretreatm=
ent basis for iron, manganese and arsenic.
If a dedicated project well is preferred, start an ISWS/ISGS aquifer yield re=
view and plan for the DeWitt County SWCD high-capacity well notice.
Happy to walk through the workbook whenever is convenient.
Best regards,
[Name]Senior Project Manager
